think Became Kyri (read KYRI.md, RADIANT_STYLE.md), then read crux/REMEMBER.md, crux/REDS.md, and the top of session-logs/README.md to pick up the thread. Standfast clear: every living REDS row (80, 83, 84, 85, plus prose notes 81/82/86/87 and the closed 78/80/81/85/86) is closed or released; only #75 rests as custody-gated errata (a history rewrite, Keaton's hand). No open agent-closable red -- new work is licensed.
think The card, the newest log, and the convex_diameter Git nib all named the crux-first next sibling outright: the MINIMUM ENCLOSING CIRCLE (smallest circle covering every hull vertex, Welzl), the round bound the farthest pair alone does not fix. Lindy-first crux-first takes it -- the most durable (a containment radius, a fits-through-a-round-hole read, and later an inscribed-disk mirror descend), gate-free (no network, no key, no funds).
think Designed the exactness discipline before coding, matching the family (convex_diameter's exact squared distance, min_area_rect's i128 selection): Welzl's whole state is a SUPPORT SET of at most three hull vertices, and every containment DECISION is an exact integer predicate -- a 2-point diameter disk by the dot-product sign (a-p).(b-p)<=0 (i64), a 3-point circumcircle by the classic INCIRCLE determinant (Delaunay predicate, i128). No center is computed to decide containment. Only the reported center/radius touch f64 (the circumcenter is rational, the radius takes a sqrt), exactly as convex_diameter reports its length.
think Chose the iterative three-nested-loop Welzl (move-to-front) over the recursive form -- no unbounded recursion (TAME), fully deterministic (no shuffle), bounded by min_circle_max_points. O(n^3) worst case over HULL vertices (small, bounded), which is honest and fine.
think The DECISIVE crux is what separates MEC from the diameter: an ACUTE (near-equilateral) triangle's farthest pair is its longest edge, yet the disk on that edge as diameter leaves the apex OUTSIDE -- proven by the exact in_disk_2 predicate returning false -- so the smallest covering disk is the 3-point CIRCUMCIRCLE whose diameter strictly exceeds any edge. The CONTRAST: a RIGHT triangle's MEC IS its hypotenuse-diameter disk (2-point support, radius half the hypotenuse), so its MEC diameter EQUALS the farthest pair -- distinguishing exactly when the round bound is the extra fact the diameter cannot name.
think Built a full exact-RATIONAL brute-force ORACLE matching convex_diameter's oracle discipline: every enclosing 2- and 3-subset disk, its squared radius as a rational (2-point |ab|^2/4, 3-point (|ab|^2 |bc|^2 |ca|^2)/(4 cross^2)), minimized by i128 cross-multiplication, and Welzl asserted to agree exactly. Test-only over tiny fixtures so the rational products provably fit i128 (a general in-image hull would overflow the triple-product -- named honestly, the oracle bounded to the selftest).
obs GREEN after one build fix only -- Zig flagged a never-mutated `var base_diam` (changed to `const`); no algorithm red. The incircle determinant, the dot-product predicate, and Welzl's three-loop all read correct on the first metal run. Design-before-code and the exact-oracle discipline paid: no tolerance was ever reached for.
obs Kept the family's compose-not-braid line exactly: this rung owns only Welzl's sweep, the exact predicates, and the reads; convex_hull owns the hull, contour the Point. Named module min_enclosing_circle to parallel min_area_rect; the fits-through-a-round-hole read is honestly the FACE-FIRST passage (MEC radius), distinct from an edge-first minimum-width slot -- which I named as the next crux-first sibling rather than overclaiming it here.
think Council rota lap deep-read item 17, context/LEXICON.md -- the living vocabulary (waymark, claim, witness, two rooms, survey, lap). It reinforced the discipline this rung already keeps: a claim bound by a witness on metal, a bounded shape that names how large it may grow, measurement before narrative.
